29:["$","li","08-passing-vs-shared",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#08-passing-vs-shared","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent/70","children":"08"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"leading-snug","children":"Message passing vs shared memory, the core trade-off"}]]}]}]
2a:["$","li","09-real-world",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#09-real-world","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent/70","children":"09"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"leading-snug","children":"IPC in the real world & why engineers care"}]]}]}]
2b:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-w-0 flex-1 flex-col gap-16","children":[["$","section","01-why-processes-talk",{"id":"01-why-processes-talk","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"01"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Why processes need to talk"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Recall the central promise of virtual memory: every process believes it owns a huge, private address space starting at address zero. Process A’s pointer 0x1000 and process B’s pointer 0x1000 refer to completely different physical memory. This is deliberate and it is enforced by hardware: A simply cannot dereference a pointer into B’s space, because the page tables that map A’s virtual addresses do not include B’s pages at all. Isolation is not a convention the processes politely honour; it is a wall the MMU refuses to let them climb."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"That wall is exactly why they cannot just share a variable. If you have two separate programs and you want one to send data to the other, there is no global they can both name, no address that means the same thing to both. The only entity that can see into both address spaces, the only one that lives on both sides of the wall, is the kernel. So every form of IPC is ultimately the kernel acting as an intermediary: either it copies bytes from one process to the other, or it arranges for a single patch of physical memory to appear in both address maps."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Those two arrangements are the two families that organise this entire chapter. It is worth fixing them in your mind before we look at any specific API, because the API is just packaging over one of these two ideas."}],["$","dl","3",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Message passing"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Processes exchange discrete messages through a kernel channel; the kernel copies the data across the boundary. Pipes, FIFOs, message queues, and sockets all live here."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Shared memory"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The kernel maps one region of physical memory into two (or more) address spaces so the processes read and write it directly, no copy, but they must synchronize themselves."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"The intermediary"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"In every case the kernel is the trusted party that bridges two otherwise isolated address spaces."}]]}]]}],"$L2d"]}],"$L2e"]}]]}],"$L2f","$L30","$L31","$L32","$L33","$L34","$L35","$L36"]}]
2c:["$","$L37",null,{"prev":{"slug":"cpu-scheduling-real","title":"CPU Scheduling II: Real Schedulers","kind":"$undefined","number":12},"next":{"slug":"race-conditions-critical-section","title":"Race Conditions & the Critical Section","kind":"$undefined","number":14}}]
38:I[76156,["2619","static/chunks/2619-04bc32f026a0d946.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","6238","static/chunks/app/operating-system/%5Bchapter%5D/page-6f5feff9906adfcd.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs"],"NoteImage"]
2d:["$","div","4",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["The one trade-off to hold",": "]}],"Message passing is simple and safe but pays a copy through the kernel on every exchange. Shared memory is blazingly fast because there is no copy, but the moment two processes touch the same bytes, you inherit every concurrency hazard we study in Part C. Speed versus simplicity: that tension runs through the whole chapter."]}]]}]
2e:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L38",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch13%2F01-why-processes-talk.jpg?v=43a0880ca55cc322decea817bac85db1","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: two process boxes (A and B), each with its own private address space drawn as a fenced-off block, unable to reach across a solid wall between them; below the wall the kernel sits with two labelled doorways through it, one marked \"message passing (copy)\" carrying an envelope, one marked \"shared memory (map)\" showing a single region wired into both processes."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
2f:["$","section","02-shared-memory",{"id":"02-shared-memory","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"02"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Shared memory, the fastest channel"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Shared memory is the fastest IPC there is, and the reason is simple: after a one-time setup, there is no kernel involvement at all. The kernel takes a region of physical memory and maps it into both processes’ page tables. From then on, when A writes a value into that region, B sees it instantly by reading the same physical bytes, no system call, no copy, no trap across the boundary. The data never moves; only the two page-table mappings point at the same place."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"On modern Linux the POSIX interface is the clean way to set this up. You call shm_open to create or open a named shared-memory object (it lives under /dev/shm), size it with ftruncate, and then mmap it into your address space. Any other process that opens the same name and mmaps it ends up pointing at the identical physical pages. The sketch below shows the producer side creating and writing the region."}],["$","figure","2",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"POSIX shared memory: create a named segment and map it in (producer side)."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"c"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"#include <sys/mman.h>   /* shm_open, mmap */\n#include <fcntl.h>     /* O_CREAT, O_RDWR */\n#include <unistd.h>    /* ftruncate */\n#include <string.h>\n\n#define SIZE 4096\n\nint main(void) {\n    int fd = shm_open(\"/demo\", O_CREAT | O_RDWR, 0600);\n    ftruncate(fd, SIZE);                    /* size the segment */\n\n    /* map the segment into our address space */\n    char *region = mmap(0, SIZE, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE,\n                        MAP_SHARED, fd, 0);\n\n    strcpy(region, \"hello from the producer\");  /* B sees this at once */\n    return 0;                                    /* region stays until shm_unlink */\n}"}]}]]}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The consumer runs the mirror image: shm_open(\"/demo\", O_RDWR, ...), mmap the same size, and read straight out of region. Because both mappings resolve to the same physical frames, the string the producer wrote is simply there. Notice what is absent, there is no read() or write() call moving the payload. That absence is the entire performance story."}],["$","p","4",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"But that same absence is the catch. Because the kernel is not mediating each exchange, it is not serialising anything either. If the producer is halfway through writing a record when the consumer reads it, the consumer gets a torn, half-updated value. This is the classic producer/consumer buffer problem, and it is unsolvable with shared memory alone. You must layer synchronization on top, a mutex or semaphore in shared memory, a lock-free ring buffer with careful memory ordering, or condition variables to signal \"data ready\". That machinery is the subject of Part C; for now, hold the warning firmly."}],"$L39"]}],"$L3a"]}]]}]
30:["$","section","03-pipes",{"id":"03-pipes","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"03"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Pipes, the shell’s workhorse"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"A pipe is the oldest and most beloved IPC mechanism in Unix, and you use it every day at the shell without thinking about it. A pipe is a unidirectional byte stream living inside the kernel: it has a write end and a read end, bytes go in one side and come out the other in order, and that is all. It carries no message boundaries, it is a raw stream of bytes, exactly like a file you can only read forward."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The classic shell pipeline is the pipe made visible. When you type ls | grep, the shell creates a pipe, then forks two processes: it wires ls’s standard output into the pipe’s write end and grep’s standard input into the read end. Neither command knows the other exists, ls just writes to fd 1 as always, grep just reads from fd 0 as always, but the kernel has quietly connected them."}],["$","figure","2",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"The pipe you use every day: ls’s output becomes grep’s input."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"# list files, keep only the .txt ones, count them\n$ ls | grep \".txt\" | wc -l\n3\n\n# ls has no idea grep exists; the kernel's pipe connects fd 1 -> fd 0"}]}]]}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Under the shell’s convenience is a system call. pipe(fd) asks the kernel for a fresh pipe and fills a two-element array: fd[0] is the read end, fd[1] is the write end. On its own that just gives one process two connected descriptors, which is not very useful. The power comes when you fork afterwards: the child inherits copies of both descriptors, and now parent and child share one pipe. Each side closes the end it does not use, and data flows across the process boundary."}],["$","figure","4",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"pipe() + fork(): the parent sends a line to the child through the kernel."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"c"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"#include <unistd.h>   /* pipe, fork, read, write, close */\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int fd[2];\n    pipe(fd);                       /* fd[0] = read end, fd[1] = write end */\n\n    if (fork() == 0) {              /* --- child: reader --- */\n        close(fd[1]);              /* child never writes */\n        char buf[64];\n        ssize_t n = read(fd[0], buf, sizeof buf);\n        write(1, buf, n);          /* echo what we received */\n        close(fd[0]);\n    } else {                        /* --- parent: writer --- */\n        close(fd[0]);              /* parent never reads */\n        write(fd[1], \"hi child\\n\", 9);\n        close(fd[1]);              /* EOF for the reader */\n    }\n    return 0;\n}"}]}]]}],"$L3b","$L3c"]}],"$L3d"]}]]}]
31:["$","section","04-named-pipes",{"id":"04-named-pipes","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"04"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Named pipes (FIFOs), pipes with a name"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"A named pipe, or FIFO, removes the one big restriction of anonymous pipes. It behaves like an ordinary pipe, unidirectional, a byte stream, first in first out, hence the name, but instead of existing only as a pair of inherited descriptors, it appears in the filesystem as a special file with a path. Any process that can open that path can join, whether or not it is related to the other end."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"You create one with the mkfifo command (or the mkfifo() system call). After that it sits in the directory listing like a file, marked with a p in ls -l, but it stores no data on disk, it is still just a kernel buffer. The filesystem entry is nothing more than a rendezvous point: a well-known name that two unrelated programs can both refer to."}],["$","figure","2",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"Two unrelated shells talking through a FIFO on disk."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"# create the named pipe once; it now shows up in ls\n$ mkfifo /tmp/mypipe\n$ ls -l /tmp/mypipe\nprw-r--r--  1 you  staff  0 Jul  9 10:00 /tmp/mypipe\n\n# terminal 1, a reader; it blocks, waiting for a writer\n$ cat /tmp/mypipe\n\n# terminal 2, an unrelated writer; its line pops out in terminal 1\n$ echo \"hello across processes\" > /tmp/mypipe"}]}]]}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Run that and something quietly remarkable happens: two shells you started separately, with no parent-child relationship at all, exchange a line of text. The reader’s cat blocks on open until a writer shows up, a FIFO open normally waits until both ends are present, and then the byte stream flows just like an anonymous pipe. The leading p in prw-r--r-- is the kernel telling you this is a pipe, not a regular file."}],["$","dl","4",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"mkfifo"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The command and system call that create a named pipe as a special file at a chosen path."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Persistent name"}],"$L3e"]}],"$L3f"]}],"$L40"]}],"$L41"]}]]}]
32:["$","section","05-message-queues",{"id":"05-message-queues","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"05"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Message queues, discrete messages, kept in order"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Pipes and FIFOs give you a byte stream: if the sender writes 10 bytes then 20 bytes, the reader might receive all 30 in one read, or 5 then 25, the boundaries are gone, and it is on you to frame the data. A message queue removes that burden. It is a kernel-managed list of discrete messages: each send deposits one whole message, each receive lifts out one whole message, and the boundaries are preserved exactly. You never have to reassemble a message from a stream."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"There are two lineages of them on Unix. The older System V message queues (msgget, msgsnd, msgrcv) are ubiquitous but clunky, identified by numeric keys. The newer POSIX message queues (mq_open, mq_send, mq_receive) are cleaner, name-based like a file, and, importantly, support message priorities: a higher-priority message jumps ahead of lower-priority ones already waiting, so urgent work is received first. The queue also persists in the kernel independently of any one process, so a sender can enqueue messages before the receiver has even started."}],["$","figure","2",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"POSIX message queue: send one discrete, prioritised message."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"c"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"#include <mqueue.h>    /* mq_open, mq_send */\n#include <fcntl.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    /* open (or create) a named queue */\n    mqd_t q = mq_open(\"/jobs\", O_CREAT | O_WRONLY, 0600, NULL);\n\n    const char *msg = \"render frame 42\";\n    unsigned int priority = 5;      /* higher = delivered sooner */\n    mq_send(q, msg, 15, priority);  /* one whole message, kept intact */\n\n    mq_close(q);\n    return 0;\n}"}]}]]}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The receiver calls mq_receive on the same \"/jobs\" name and gets back that message as a single unit, together with its priority, never a half message, never two messages fused together. That framing, plus priorities, plus kernel persistence, is what you are paying for."}],["$","div","4",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["When you would choose them",": "]}],"Reach for a message queue when the natural unit of work is a discrete task or event, when you want the kernel to preserve message boundaries for you, and when priority ordering matters. That said, in modern distributed systems most teams reach past OS-level queues for a networked broker: RabbitMQ, Kafka, an SQS, because those cross machines and add durability. OS message queues remain the right, low-overhead choice for structured messaging strictly between processes on one host."]}]]}]]}],"$L42"]}]]}]
33:["$","section","06-signals",{"id":"06-signals","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"06"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Signals, asynchronous notifications"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Signals are the odd member of the IPC family. They are not a channel for data, they are asynchronous notifications, tiny interrupts the kernel delivers to a process to say \"something happened\". A signal carries essentially no payload; it is just a number with a well-known meaning. When one arrives, the process is yanked out of whatever it was doing, runs a short handler if it has installed one, and then resumes. It is the software equivalent of a hardware interrupt."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"You have sent signals by hand many times. Pressing Ctrl-C in a terminal sends SIGINT to the foreground process. The kill command, despite its name, is a general signal sender, kill -TERM 1234 asks process 1234 to shut down gracefully. A few signals you meet constantly:"}],["$","dl","2",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"SIGINT"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Interrupt from the keyboard (Ctrl-C). The default action is to terminate, but a program can catch it to clean up first."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"SIGTERM"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A polite request to terminate. Catchable, so a process can finish work, flush data, and exit cleanly. The default \"please stop\" signal."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"SIGKILL"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Terminate immediately and unconditionally. Cannot be caught, blocked, or ignored, the kernel does it for you. The last resort."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"SIGCHLD"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Sent to a parent when a child stops or exits, so the parent can wait() on it and reap the zombie."}]]}]]}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"A process decides how to respond by installing a signal handler. The example below catches SIGTERM so that, instead of dying on the spot, the program gets a chance to run cleanup code first, the foundation of graceful shutdown."}],["$","figure","4",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":["$L43","$L44"]}],"$L45"]}],"$L46"]}],"$L47"]}]]}]
34:["$","section","07-sockets",{"id":"07-sockets","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"07"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Sockets, the general-purpose channel"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Sockets are the most general and most widely used IPC mechanism, because a single API covers two situations at once: communication between processes on the same machine, and communication between processes on different machines across a network. You learn one set of calls, socket, bind, listen, accept, connect, send, recv, and it works whether the peer is next door or across the planet. Unlike a pipe, a socket is naturally bidirectional: both ends can send and receive."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"When both processes are on the same host, you use a Unix-domain socket. It has an address that is a filesystem path, something like /tmp/app.sock, and the kernel keeps all the data inside the machine, never touching the network stack. This makes it fast and private, and it is the standard way local services expose an endpoint: your Docker daemon listens on /var/run/docker.sock, and many databases offer a Unix-domain socket for local clients as a quicker alternative to a TCP connection."}],["$","dl","2",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Unix-domain socket"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A socket addressed by a filesystem path, for processes on the same host. Bidirectional, fast, and never leaves the machine, the local-IPC option."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Network socket"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A socket addressed by IP and port, for processes on different machines, riding TCP or UDP. Same API, now spanning the network."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"One interface, two reaches"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The socket API abstracts \"where the peer is\" so the same code serves local and remote communication."}]]}]]}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"When the peer is on another machine, you use a network socket addressed by IP and port, carried over TCP or UDP. The code barely changes, you swap the address family and the address, and the same send and recv move your bytes. That is the quiet genius of the design: the difference between \"talk to the process next door\" and \"talk to a server in another datacentre\" is a few lines of setup, not a different mental model."}],"$L48"]}],"$L49"]}]]}]
35:["$","section","08-passing-vs-shared",{"id":"08-passing-vs-shared","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"08"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Message passing vs shared memory, the core trade-off"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Now we can put the two families side by side, because almost every IPC decision comes down to choosing between them. In message passing, pipes, FIFOs, message queues, sockets, the kernel copies data from the sender’s buffer into its own, then into the receiver’s buffer. That copy costs time and it happens on every exchange. But it buys you three big things: the kernel serialises access so you never see a torn value, the model is simple to reason about, and, crucially for sockets, the very same model extends across machines, because copying to a remote process is conceptually no different from copying to a local one."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Shared memory inverts every one of those properties. There is no per-exchange copy, so it is the fastest option by a wide margin, the data simply sits in memory both processes can see. But nothing serialises access for you, so you must add synchronization yourself and get it exactly right, and the mechanism is inherently confined to a single machine, since you cannot map one physical memory region into a process on another host."}],["$","dl","2",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Speed"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Shared memory wins decisively: no kernel copy after setup. Message passing pays a copy across the boundary on every send."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Simplicity & safety"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Message passing wins: the kernel serialises exchanges, so there are no torn reads. Shared memory hands you the concurrency problem to solve yourself."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Copying vs synchronization"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The real trade is what work you do: message passing spends CPU copying bytes; shared memory spends your engineering effort on locks and correct memory ordering."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Distributed-friendliness"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Message passing (via sockets) spans machines unchanged; shared memory is stuck on one host, forever."}]]}]]}],"$L4a"]}],"$L4b"]}]]}]
36:["$","section","09-real-world",{"id":"09-real-world","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"09"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"IPC in the real world & why engineers care"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"None of this is academic, the systems you run every day are held together by exactly these mechanisms, and being able to name them changes how you debug. Consider where each one shows up."}],["$","ul","1",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Pipes are the soul of the Unix shell: every command1 | command2 you type wires two processes together through a kernel pipe, which is why small, single-purpose tools compose into powerful one-liners."}]]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Sockets are everywhere, every web request, database connection, and microservice call is a socket. Local tools use Unix-domain sockets: the Docker CLI talks to its daemon over /var/run/docker.sock, and browsers reach local dev servers over TCP."}]]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Shared memory powers high-performance systems where copy cost is unacceptable. PostgreSQL keeps its shared buffer pool, the cache of disk pages, in a shared-memory segment that every backend process maps in, so a page read by one connection is instantly available to all."}]]}],["$","li","3",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Signals drive lifecycle and graceful shutdown. When Kubernetes or Docker stops a container, it sends SIGTERM first and waits; a well-behaved server catches it, stops accepting new work, drains in-flight requests, and exits. Ignore SIGTERM and the orchestrator eventually follows with an uncatchable SIGKILL and cuts you off mid-request."}]]}]]}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"That last point is worth dwelling on because it bites teams constantly. A container that does not handle SIGTERM will appear to \"lose\" requests during every deploy, because it is being hard-killed rather than draining. The fix is pure IPC: install a SIGTERM handler, flip a shutdown flag, and finish gracefully, precisely the pattern from the signals section. Knowing IPC turns a mysterious deploy-time error into a five-line fix."}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Step back and the chapter forms one picture. Processes are isolated by design, so the kernel offers channels to bridge them, and those channels sort into two families: message passing, where the kernel copies discrete data across the wall (pipes, FIFOs, message queues, sockets), and shared memory, where one region is mapped into two address spaces for zero-copy speed at the price of synchronization. Signals sit apart as lightweight control notifications. Pick the mechanism by matching its trade-offs to your problem: reach, speed, message framing, and how much synchronization you are willing to own."}],"$L4c"]}],"$L4d"]}]]}]
39:["$","div","5",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Why engineers care",": "]}],"When two processes on one machine must exchange large volumes of data at the lowest possible latency, a database sharing its buffer pool across backends, a video pipeline passing frames, shared memory is the tool. You trade away the kernel’s built-in safety for raw speed, and you pay for it in synchronization code you must get exactly right."]}]]}]
3a:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L38",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch13%2F02-shared-memory.jpg?v=7261c9786a4a7e46208ac9ce1bc721a3","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: a single physical RAM region in the middle labelled \"shared segment\"; arrows map it into process A’s address space on the left and process B’s address space on the right via mmap, so both point at the same physical frames; a producer arrow writes into a ring buffer inside the region and a consumer arrow reads from it, with a small padlock icon labelled \"you must add synchronization\" hovering over the shared bytes."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
3b:["$","p","5",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Two details in that code are the whole discipline of pipes. First, each side closes the end it will not use, the parent closes the read end, the child closes the write end, because the reader only gets end-of-file once every write end is closed. Forget to close, and the reader blocks forever waiting for data that will never come. Second, the direction is fixed: this pipe carries data parent-to-child only. If you need to talk back, you create a second pipe going the other way."}]
3c:["$","div","6",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["The key limitation",": "]}],"Anonymous pipes only connect related processes, a parent and the children it forks, because the connection is shared by inheriting descriptors across fork. Two unrelated programs, started independently, have no descriptor to inherit, so they cannot use an anonymous pipe. That gap is exactly what the next mechanism fills."]}]]}]
3d:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L38",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch13%2F03-pipes.jpg?v=cfb031c45944c2a863c642c808bee1ab","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: a fork creating a parent and child process; between them a kernel-managed pipe drawn as a tube with a write end (fd[1]) on the left process and a read end (fd[0]) on the right; bytes flow one direction only, left to right, through the tube; below, the shell command \"ls | grep .txt\" with an arrow showing ls’s stdout wired into grep’s stdin."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
3e:["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The FIFO’s path lives in the filesystem until removed, so processes can find it by name across time, but no data is stored on disk."}]
3f:["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Connects strangers"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Unlike anonymous pipes, unrelated processes can meet at a FIFO because both open the same well-known path."}]]}]
40:["$","div","5",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Where you meet it",": "]}],"FIFOs are a lightweight way to bolt two independent programs together without writing socket code, a long-running script that reads commands from /tmp/control, or a quick logging hookup between tools. They are still one-directional byte streams, so for anything richer you reach for message queues or sockets."]}]]}]
41:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L38",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch13%2F04-named-pipes.jpg?v=25dc38a7f4a0b23c5f24bab85cda1069","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: a filesystem path /tmp/mypipe drawn as a special file icon labelled \"FIFO\"; on the left an unrelated writer process (a shell running echo) opens the path and pushes bytes in; on the right a separate, unrelated reader process opens the same path and pulls bytes out; a note points to the file saying \"persists on disk, connects strangers\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
42:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L38",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch13%2F05-message-queues.jpg?v=d022633615a59206e056ccf10ba3fd1e","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: a kernel-owned queue drawn as a stack of distinct envelopes (each a whole message, not a byte stream), some tagged with a priority number; a sender process on the left drops envelopes in with mq_send, a receiver on the right lifts them out with mq_receive, highest-priority envelope taken first; a label notes \"message boundaries preserved\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
43:["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"Catching SIGTERM to shut down gracefully instead of dying instantly."}]
44:["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"c"}]
45:["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"#include <signal.h>\n#include <unistd.h>\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nstatic volatile sig_atomic_t stop = 0;\n\nvoid on_term(int sig) { stop = 1; }   /* just flip a flag; keep handlers tiny */\n\nint main(void) {\n    signal(SIGTERM, on_term);          /* install our handler */\n\n    while (!stop) {\n        /* ... do real work ... */\n        sleep(1);\n    }\n    printf(\"caught SIGTERM, cleaning up\\n\");  /* graceful exit path */\n    return 0;\n}"}]}]
46:["$","div","5",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["The limit that defines them",": "]}],"A signal tells a process that something happened, but not what data goes with it, there is no message body, and if two of the same signal arrive close together they may be coalesced into one. Signals are perfect for control and lifecycle events (\"stop\", \"reload your config\", \"a child exited\") and wrong for moving data. For that, use one of the channels in this chapter."]}]]}]
47:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L38",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch13%2F06-signals.jpg?v=d16d1015faae93a0ba66b72f8f9f6d0a","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: one process sending a signal to another via the kill() call; the signal drawn as a lightning bolt labelled SIGTERM striking the target process, which jumps out of its normal flow into a small \"signal handler\" box and then returns; a side list of common signals (SIGINT from Ctrl-C, SIGTERM, SIGKILL \"cannot be caught\", SIGCHLD) with a note \"no data payload, just the number\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
48:["$","div","4",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Bridge to the Computer Networking course",": "]}],"Network sockets are where operating systems hand off to networking. How those bytes actually cross the wire: TCP’s reliable stream, the three-way handshake, IP routing, ports and addresses, is the whole subject of the Computer Networking notes, so we will not duplicate it here. For this chapter, the takeaway is that a socket is the process’s doorway onto that machinery, exposed through the same IPC API you use locally."]}]]}]
49:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L38",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch13%2F07-sockets.jpg?v=a965fc296bc3836141533535b5681cbf","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: two panels sharing the same socket API. Left panel \"same machine\": two processes connected by a Unix-domain socket through a path /tmp/app.sock, bytes staying inside the kernel. Right panel \"across machines\": two processes on separate boxes connected by a network socket over TCP/IP, arrow crossing a cloud labelled network; a banner over both reads \"one API, socket(), bind(), connect(), send(), recv()\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
4a:["$","div","3",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["How to choose",": "]}],"Default to message passing, usually sockets, because it is simpler, safer, and scales from one machine to many without changing your design. Reach for shared memory only when profiling proves the copy cost is your bottleneck and both processes are guaranteed to be on the same host, and then budget real time for getting the synchronization right."]}]]}]
4b:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L38",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch13%2F08-passing-vs-shared.jpg?v=56d26cbe72335d4f840f280e6bf75ee3","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: a two-column comparison table. Left column \"message passing\" shows process A → kernel copy → process B with two arrows crossing the boundary and a tick beside \"kernel synchronizes, simple, works across machines\". Right column \"shared memory\" shows one region wired into both A and B directly with a tick beside \"no copy, fastest\" and a warning beside \"you synchronize, one machine only\". A balance scale at the bottom weighs speed against simplicity."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
4c:["$","div","4",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["What is next",": "]}],"Shared memory left us with a dangling promise: the moment two processes touch the same bytes, we inherit race conditions, and the fixes, mutexes, semaphores, condition variables, are their own deep subject. That is exactly where Part C on concurrency and synchronization picks up."]}]]}]
4d:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L38",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch13%2F09-real-world.jpg?v=18dc59fe683c87e3d626047aace6a764","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: four labelled vignettes around a central \"IPC\" hub, a shell pipeline (cmd | cmd) tagged \"pipes\", two servers over a network tagged \"sockets everywhere\", a database engine with a shared buffer pool tagged \"shared memory (Postgres)\", and a container receiving a SIGTERM lightning bolt from an orchestrator tagged \"graceful shutdown\"; arrows tie each vignette back to the mechanism it uses."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
